---
scope: src/tools/init
intent: >
  aide_init is the bootstrap — the one command that turns a cold
  project into an AIDE-ready project. It auto-detects the host agent
  framework, installs a short AIDE pointer stub into the framework's
  config file, lands the progressively-disclosed methodology doc hub
  onto the host's disk alongside it, scaffolds a slash command for
  every pipeline phase, wires the MCP server into the project's MCP
  config, and configures the IDE so `.aide` files render as Markdown.
  Every step is idempotent so the tool is safe to re-run, and every
  step reports its own status so the caller can see exactly what
  changed.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Auto-detection picks the right framework (Claude Code, Cursor,
      Windsurf, Copilot-in-VSCode) from files already on disk, and the
      caller can override the detection explicitly when they need to.
    - The framework's canonical config file (e.g. CLAUDE.md for
      Claude Code) receives a short marker-bounded pointer stub
      exactly once — a handful of lines that name the host-side doc
      hub and tell the agent when to crawl it. Subsequent runs detect
      the stub's marker and skip the write.
    - The host-side doc hub — a dedicated folder containing every
      canonical methodology doc verbatim plus a hub index that links
      to each one — is installed alongside the stub. The pointer in
      the stub and the path the hub installs into are derived from a
      single shared source, so the two sides cannot disagree. Agents
      in the host project follow the stub's pointer to the hub and
      crawl from there into whichever canonical doc the current task
      requires, the same way `study-playbook` works against the
      user's global brain.
    - The six pipeline slash commands (research, spec, plan, build,
      qa, fix) are scaffolded into an `aide/` subfolder of the host
      framework's command directory, one file per phase, composed
      from the canonical command templates under `docs/commands/aide/`.
      The subfolder is load-bearing: host frameworks derive the slash
      command namespace from folder nesting, so installing
      `<command-dir>/aide/research.md` produces `/aide:research`.
      These commands are the entire runtime of the AIDE pipeline in
      the host project — the architect, the builder, the QA agent,
      the fix agents, the research agent all exist because init put
      them there. Existing command files are left untouched; only
      missing ones are created.
    - The MCP config is patched to register the `aide` server. If the
      file exists and parses, the server is added to its `mcpServers`
      map; if the file is missing, a minimal config is created. An
      existing `aide` entry is left alone.
    - IDE configuration runs by default and can be skipped with a
      flag. Zed gets a `.zed/settings.json` patch; VS Code gets the
      bundled `.vsix` installed through the `code` CLI when that CLI
      is available. Missing CLI is a skip, not an error.
    - Every step's status — created, wired, installed, exists,
      skipped — is reported in the final summary. A fully-cold run
      reads as a list of creates; a fully-warm run reads as a list of
      "already exists".
    - Re-running the tool on a project that is already fully
      initialized returns a summary saying so and changes nothing on
      disk.
  undesired:
    - An init that appends the pointer stub to the config file on
      every run, so a project initialized three times contains three
      stacked copies of the marker-bounded region. Idempotency is an
      invariant, not a nice-to-have.
    - An init that ships the full concatenated methodology body
      inside the config file's marker-bounded region instead of a
      short pointer stub. Every non-AIDE session in the host project
      pays the cost of carrying the full doctrine on every read of
      the config file, which is exactly the delivery-layer violation
      of progressive disclosure this spec exists to reverse.
    - An init whose pointer stub names a host-side doc-hub path that
      disagrees with where the doc-hub installer actually writes the
      hub. The two sides derive from a single shared source; any
      arrangement that lets them drift by editing one side without
      the other is a design regression.
    - Slash command content, stub teaching text, or canonical
      methodology content hand-written as template literals inside
      this submodule's source instead of composed from the canonical
      docs under `docs/`. The source of truth is in `docs/`; anything
      else is a drifting duplicate.
    - An init that overwrites an existing slash command because "the
      template is the canonical version". The user may have customized
      it. Customizations survive init.
    - An init that clobbers the `mcpServers` map when it fails to
      parse the existing MCP config, destroying every other server
      the user had configured. Parse failure is a skip, not a rewrite.
    - An init that fails the whole run when any single step fails. IDE
      configuration failing because `code` is not on PATH should not
      prevent the stub, the doc hub, the commands, or the MCP wiring
      from being written.
    - An init that reaches for the network — downloading a `.vsix`,
      fetching a template, contacting a marketplace. The tool must
      succeed offline, because the developer running it may have no
      network and because network calls turn a local operation into a
      flaky one.
    - An init whose success output is a single word ("done") or a
      serialized status object. The caller needs to see exactly which
      steps created files and which were skipped, because that is
      the information they will paste into a commit or cite to a
      teammate.
---

## Context

Every new project that adopts AIDE has to cross the same cold-start
gap: an entry-point pointer to AIDE in the surface the agent already
reads on every session, the full methodology installed somewhere the
agent can crawl on demand, pipeline slash commands scaffolded, the
MCP server registered, and the IDE taught to render `.aide` as
Markdown. Without a single tool that does all of this, each project
rediscovers the list and gets one or two steps wrong — a stub written
to the wrong file, a doc hub installed to a path the stub doesn't
name, a slash command with a typo, an MCP config that registers the
server under the wrong name, an IDE that still renders `.aide` as
plain text. This tool exists to collapse the cold-start gap into one
command, so the decision to adopt AIDE in a new project is trivially
reversible and costs nothing to try.

Methodology delivery inside this tool is split across two peer helpers
on purpose. One installs the short pointer stub into the host's config
file so the agent learns in its very first read of the session that
AIDE exists and where the full material lives. The other installs the
full material — the canonical methodology docs plus a hub index — into
a dedicated doc-hub folder on the host's disk, so the agent reads only
what the current task requires and only when the task requires it.
The split mirrors the methodology's own progressive-disclosure rule at
the delivery layer: the high-traffic surface teaches where to look,
the low-traffic surface holds the actual content.

The tool composes private helpers that live as subfolders of this
orchestrator: framework detection, methodology and command template
content, the four idempotent write steps (`writeMethodology`,
`installMethodologyDocs`, `scaffoldCommands`, `wireMcp`), and IDE
configuration. Init itself is thin — it orchestrates those helpers,
sequences their results, and shapes the final summary. The strategic
decisions about *how* to configure each IDE, or *which* files count
as a framework's canonical config, live next to the helpers that
make those decisions.

## Strategy

**Each step is independently idempotent and independently reportable.**
Every step — methodology write, command scaffold, MCP wire, Zed patch,
VS Code install — knows how to detect its own "already done" state and
reports `exists` when it finds one. The steps do not share state, so a
step that fails or skips cannot corrupt the steps around it, and the
final summary can honestly report a mix of creates and skips in the
same run.

**Failures in one step do not abort the others.** IDE configuration is
the canonical example: a developer without the `code` CLI should still
get a fully-configured methodology, commands, and MCP wiring. The tool
treats each step as best-effort and surfaces the mix of outcomes in
the summary. The alternative — abort on first failure — would make
init brittle in exactly the environments where it needs to be
reliable.

**Host framework detection is a read-only inspection.** The detector
looks at files already on disk (a `.claude` directory, a `cursor`
config, a `.vscode/settings.json` with Copilot-ish keys) and returns
the first match. Nothing is written during detection, and the caller
can always override the result with an explicit `framework` parameter.
This keeps detection safe to run speculatively and makes the override
path a first-class escape hatch.

**Network is never the happy path.** Every asset init needs is checked
into the repo — the methodology text, the command templates, the
bundled `.vsix`. A developer running init on a laptop with no network
gets the same result as a developer running it online. Anything that
would require a network round-trip is a design bug in this tool, not
a feature to add.

**Report the full transition, not just success or failure.** The
summary names each step, labels its status, and orders them in
execution order. The caller reads the summary once and knows exactly
what changed on disk, which files to re-read, and which steps were
already done. A cold run and a warm run produce the same shape of
output — the only difference is which status words appear.

## Good examples

A developer runs `aide_init` on a fresh project in Claude Code and
sees:

> AIDE initialized (claude framework):
>
>   ✓ Methodology pointer: Created
>   ✓ .aide/aide-spec.md: Created
>   ✓ .aide/aide-template.md: Created
>   ✓ .aide/progressive-disclosure.md: Created
>   ✓ .aide/agent-readable-code.md: Created
>   ✓ .aide/automated-qa.md: Created
>   ✓ .aide/index.md: Created
>   ✓ aide/research: Created
>   ✓ aide/spec: Created
>   ✓ aide/plan: Created
>   ✓ aide/build: Created
>   ✓ aide/qa: Created
>   ✓ aide/fix: Created
>   ✓ MCP config: Wired
>   ✓ Zed config: Created
>   ✓ VS Code extension: Installed
>
> Config: CLAUDE.md
> Doc hub: .aide
> Commands: .claude/commands/aide
> MCP: .mcp.json
>
> New commands: /aide:research, /aide:spec, /aide:plan, /aide:build, /aide:qa, /aide:fix
>
> Next steps: run aide_discover to see existing specs, or /aide:research to start a new one.

Every step accounted for, every path named, and a pointer to the next
action. The developer reads one screen and knows the project is ready.

A developer re-runs the same command on the same project and sees:

> AIDE already initialized (claude framework detected).
>
> All components present:
>   - Methodology pointer
>   - .aide/aide-spec.md
>   - .aide/aide-template.md
>   - .aide/progressive-disclosure.md
>   - .aide/agent-readable-code.md
>   - .aide/automated-qa.md
>   - .aide/index.md
>   - aide/research
>   - aide/spec
>   - aide/plan
>   - aide/build
>   - aide/qa
>   - aide/fix
>   - MCP config
>   - Zed config
>   - VS Code extension
>
> Run aide_discover to see existing specs.

Nothing on disk changed, and the output says so plainly. The re-run
was safe.

A developer without the `code` CLI runs `aide_init` and the VS Code
step is reported as `Skipped` while every other step still runs to
completion. They install `code`, re-run init, and the VS Code step
flips to `Installed` while every other step reports `Already exists`.
The tool is composable with the developer's environment, not
adversarial to it.

## Bad examples

An init that appends the pointer stub to CLAUDE.md on every run,
producing a file with three stacked marker-bounded regions after
three runs. The idempotency invariant has been violated and the
config file is now corrupted.

An init whose stub names a host-side doc hub at `.aide/` while the
installer writes the hub into `aide/`. The two sides shipped with
coincident string literals instead of a shared path source; the
agent follows the stub, lands on an empty directory, and concludes
AIDE is broken. The refactor technically passed every test and
failed its only load-bearing invariant.

An init that installs a full concatenated methodology body into
CLAUDE.md "because it's simpler than landing a whole doc hub on
disk". The stub shape was skipped, progressive disclosure at the
delivery layer was skipped, and every non-AIDE session in the host
pays the full-body cost on every read. The regression this design
exists to reverse has been reintroduced.

An init that overwrites `.claude/commands/aide/build.md` with the
bundled template, wiping out the user's customizations. The tool has
no way to know what the customizations were for, and the user finds
out by reading a diff they did not expect.

An init that catches a parse error on the existing `.mcp.json` and
responds by writing a fresh minimal config, destroying the user's
other MCP server registrations. The right move is to skip the wire
step and report the skip in the summary.

An init that aborts the whole run because `code --version` exited
non-zero. The developer wanted a configured methodology, commands,
and MCP config; what they got was an error and an unconfigured
project, because one optional step was missing an optional
dependency.

An init whose summary reads `done`. The developer has no idea which
files changed, whether the methodology was written, whether the
slash commands were new or pre-existing, or which IDE the tool
configured. The single word is worse than no output, because it
implies a report was generated.
